00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.800
So before we get into the new
part, let's talk about what

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.560
Cloud Spanner is, what
have we been doing so far?

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.370
So we launched Cloud Spanner
externally on the GCP platform

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.320
last year on Valentine's Day.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.670
And then we quickly went
to general availability.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.120
And in the last 14
or so months, we've

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.810
seen some great traction on it.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:05.239
But before Cloud
Spanner was there,

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:10.190
you had to choose what database
you wanted based on the one

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.617
really important
attribute of your database

00:01:12.617 --> 00:01:13.700
that you couldn't give up.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.780
So either it was
performance, or scale,

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:21.170
or SQL, or relational semantics,
however you want to put it,

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:22.810
or manageability, right?

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.530
Or, or, or, or, or.

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:28.460
And with Cloud
Spanner, basically, you

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.980
don't have to make those
compromises anymore.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.890
It is our no compromise
database that

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:36.670
combines the benefits
of both horizontal scale

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:40.520
and performance and the
familiar relational semantics

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:46.010
that you're used to with SQL NC
2011 compliance SQL dialects,

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.254
table schemas--

00:01:47.254 --> 00:01:48.920
everything you're
used to and like about

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.200
relational databases.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.210
So we launched and are currently
available in 13 GCP regions.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:59.600
We also have multi-region
configurations.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:01.470
And they give you
four and five nines

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:05.180
of availability, which
means five nines really

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.740
means less than five
minutes of downtime a year.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.690
So if you've gone
upstairs and seen

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.830
in Moscone West, the fifth
nine-- which is our SRE lounge,

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.290
it's worth visiting--
but basically, we

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:20.150
are highly reliable, highly
available database service

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.490
offered on Google
Cloud platform.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:27.560
So why Cloud Spanner?

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.220
And how are we releasing
it to the world?

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.200
So as I said, Spanner
combines the benefits

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.910
of many different
types of systems

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.460
so that you don't have to make
those compromises anymore.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.250
But databases have
existed for 30 years.

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:44.630
So we could have started to
add a whole bunch of features

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.830
to our database that
we launched last year,

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:51.680
but we wanted to be
deliberate about how

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:56.600
we go about adding features
and functionality to Spanner.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.630
Our philosophy with Cloud
Spanner from the beginning

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:02.760
has been building for
you, for our customers.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.690
We had been using this system
internally for multiple years,

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.200
so it's not something that
we picked up and threw over

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.060
the fence and said,
hey, have fun with it.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.970
It's not just some cool tech,
but it's actually useful.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.830
And we launched with our basic
functionality that we did.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.010
And then we wanted to
add features as we go,

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:23.820
based on how customers
perceived it and wanted from it.

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:27.110
So we have been deliberate,
and that's something

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:29.510
that I'll talk about as we go.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:32.141
But you should tell us what
you want us to build next.

00:03:32.141 --> 00:03:33.890
So you can find me on
LinkedIn, obviously.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.820
But please tell us
on Stack Overflow,

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.350
on those two headings.

00:03:39.350 --> 00:03:43.740
And we'd love to know what more
you'd like from the system.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.320
So as I said, we launched
about 14-ish months ago.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.470
And what have we been doing
as we're being deliberate

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:53.300
about adding functionality?

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.490
So we added multi-region
configurations.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:58.080
Again, this is something
that's very popular internally,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.500
but we wanted to make
sure that we provided

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.240
this functionality
only it was useful

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.900
to our external customers.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:06.390
And it turns out it was a
very high-demand feature,

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:09.080
so we added it late last year.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.790
We also have admin and
data access logging.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.900
We have Stackdriver
integrations and metrics

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.040
and more metrics coming.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.959
We keep adding metrics
based on what you all

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:20.627
are looking for,
rather than, oh, here's

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:22.460
a whole bunch of metrics,
and now figure out

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.680
how to tune your
system based on it.

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.059
We have a very cool
feature, again,

00:04:27.059 --> 00:04:28.600
that was internally
used, but also it

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:30.540
looks like it's very
useful externally,

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.920
which is commit timestamps, that
we launched earlier this year.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:39.440
And that allows you to
basically log transactionally

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:42.410
when a transaction committed.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:45.110
So as part of a
transaction to a row,

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:47.290
you can actually
write the timestamp

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.580
which is that
transaction committed,

00:04:49.580 --> 00:04:51.890
which allows you to
build many, many things,

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:54.920
like change logs, triggers,
change [INAUDIBLE] capture,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.330
et cetera, on top of it.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:58.970
We also have open
census integration

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.790
for more debugging, again,
and then, of course,

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:02.960
compliance certification.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.656
And the list goes on, but
these are some of the things

00:05:05.656 --> 00:05:07.280
that, again, we're
asked for, and we've

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.190
tried to deliver deliberately.

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:13.490
So before we move
on, Cloud Spanner

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.110
has seen tremendous
adoption across all industry

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:20.030
and across all verticals.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.750
And I'm so excited to have one
of our very cool customers here

00:05:23.750 --> 00:05:27.540
today from Belgium to tell
us more about their use case.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:29.084
Mark is from
nexuzhealth, and he's

00:05:29.084 --> 00:05:30.500
going to tell us
a little bit more

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:31.890
about how they're using Spanner.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:32.540
So Mark?

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:32.950
MARK VANAUTGAERDEN: Hi.

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.283
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Take it away.

00:05:34.283 --> 00:05:36.318
MARK VANAUTGAERDEN: Thank you.

00:05:36.318 --> 00:05:38.421
So hello.

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:41.080
So my name is Mark,
Mark Vanautgaerden.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:43.060
I'm from nexuzhealth,
and I'll just

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.830
give you a little overview of
what we are and what we do.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.920
So probably you don't know us--

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:52.600
Belgium, small country
somewhere in Europe.

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:54.820
We played fairly well
in the Cup, they say.

00:05:54.820 --> 00:05:58.480
And in Belgium, there is a
large University Hospital.

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.510
It's called UZ Leuven.

00:06:00.510 --> 00:06:03.280
It's quite a large university
and training hospital.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:08.680
It's about 2,000 beds and
about 9,000 medical staff.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.780
And there is all centralized IT.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:14.620
So we do everything centralized,
so nothing is decentralized.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.820
And we do it with about
180 hats, currently.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:21.670
Over the last 25
years, we've been

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:25.600
building our own internal
electronic medical records

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:26.490
system.

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:29.560
And that we call internally
the clinical work station.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:31.780
And it's what was important
from the very beginning

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:34.210
that it's the one stop
for everything you

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:36.520
do with the patient data.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.100
So not just for
physicians, but also

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:41.110
for the nursing, for the
administrative people,

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:43.760
et cetera, everybody inside.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.950
It's patient-centric, and it's
very, very workflow oriented.

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.480
So it has a big burden on
workflow also on the database.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.580
And so all of it for
those who are familiar,

00:06:55.580 --> 00:06:57.620
gives lots of
functionality, not just

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.720
the classical medical report,
but lots of older thing,

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:05.290
like planning, and order
entry, and drugs prescription,

00:07:05.290 --> 00:07:07.460
and administration,
et cetera, et cetera--

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.490
so a complete patient
management system.

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:13.930
So that was quite successful
over the last 25 years.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:15.760
And we rolled it
out gradually in

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.420
that large academic hospital.

00:07:19.420 --> 00:07:22.600
It was so successful that
a lot of other hospitals

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:26.204
in the region said, whoa, why
can't we have that system?

00:07:26.204 --> 00:07:27.370
Why don't you sell it to us?

00:07:27.370 --> 00:07:30.070
And we said, well, we
don't sell it, yet.

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.360
At that moment, that
was 10 years ago.

00:07:32.360 --> 00:07:34.690
So we said, why don't
you just join us.

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:37.405
And we're not going to do to
sell it and give you the CD

00:07:37.405 --> 00:07:40.630
and install it with you, just
join us in the same database

00:07:40.630 --> 00:07:43.150
and act like you're
working in our hospital,

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:44.860
but just on another side.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:49.310
So at that time, it was just by
accident that we said, well, go

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.760
and [INAUDIBLE]
at one big system.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.590
But gradually, it evolved in
a very important thing, namely

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.890
that we built one
central medical file

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:02.110
across the complete
region of Flanders

00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:04.480
with all patient data
in there, centrally,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:07.180
without having the
difficulty of connecting

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.140
between all different systems.

00:08:09.140 --> 00:08:11.840
So it means that it's
a very big offering.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:18.010
So we optimize across hospitals,
workflows, and data flow.

00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:19.750
So whenever you
enter data here, it's

00:08:19.750 --> 00:08:21.226
automatically at the other side.

00:08:21.226 --> 00:08:22.600
And there's no
data going across.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.940
It's a big advantage
in Flanders.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.030
So currently, there's about 30
hospitals connected to that.

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:31.330
21 are currently
in full production.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:33.490
The other ones are
still connected to it.

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.210
So as I said, it's one
big shared medical record.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:41.799
That currently, about 13,000
beds managed in that system.

00:08:41.799 --> 00:08:45.130
And that are 30,000 named
users in the system.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:50.080
And every three minutes, there
are about 6,000 users active

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.100
in the database.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:53.040
So that is the current set up.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:55.690
And well, we grow fast
over the last year.

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:58.360
For those who are a bit
familiar with the geography,

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:00.400
this is Belgium, northern part.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.790
So some of you will recognize in
the center, there is Brussels.

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:05.110
Some of you have heard of that.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:07.900
And also, there is something
like Antwerp Ghent, and Bruges.

00:09:07.900 --> 00:09:09.200
You're familiar with that.

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.070
So over the last 10
years, all of a sudden,

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:15.130
we were across Flanders,
and across Flanders

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:20.090
that when you live at
the sea, at the coast,

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:22.630
and you have a very big
problem, medical problem,

00:09:22.630 --> 00:09:26.170
you are sent to that
big central hospital

00:09:26.170 --> 00:09:28.270
somewhere around
Brussels in Leuven.

00:09:28.270 --> 00:09:31.300
And they automatically have
all your medical data there.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:32.620
And it's a big plus.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.620
And afterwards,
you go back home,

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.110
and all data is transferred
automagically again,

00:09:38.110 --> 00:09:39.654
and it's a big plus.

00:09:39.654 --> 00:09:40.570
But what does it mean?

00:09:40.570 --> 00:09:45.280
We currently have about 50%
of the Belgium patients beds

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:48.070
and hospitals in that system.

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:50.830
Also, it's all in
one big database.

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:51.600
Well, wow.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:56.120
And it does about 1 million
plus transactions a day.

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:58.630
So that is that setup.

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:01.330
The current architecture
is quite, quite classical.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.430
So we do it all ourselves.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:06.830
It's basically a
client-server architecture.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.490
So with a very big,
one central instance

00:10:09.490 --> 00:10:12.310
database in the middle
that is core to everything,

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:15.490
and then connected, of
course, to the clients--

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:19.010
either the laptops, or there
is mobile applications,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:21.141
or there is e-health
connections, et cetera,

00:10:21.141 --> 00:10:21.640
et cetera.

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:25.240
But sole driven by
that one, big database.

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:28.090
As you can imagine,
we grow a bit fast.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:31.580
And it wasn't envisioned
to be that big.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:35.080
So there is a big risk on that.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:36.390
And what do we need?

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:40.010
We said, OK, let's look
for a new solution that

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:43.050
is more future-proof and that
we can grow even further.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.170
But we have a lot of
constraints, currently.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.970
And as you can see, size
is an important stuff.

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:50.270
So of course, we are
about 14 terabytes

00:10:50.270 --> 00:10:51.740
of online data in there.

00:10:51.740 --> 00:10:55.100
We do not do any
archiving in there.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.290
Availability, of
course, for a hospital--

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:58.760
very, very important.

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:01.580
You could live with the
computer is out for a minute

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:03.950
during a very important surgery.

00:11:03.950 --> 00:11:05.010
But 10 minutes?

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:06.800
Whoa, that will be a problem.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.330
So you will not have that.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.610
Scalability in our environment
is really important

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:13.250
because those 30 hospitals
will not be the end of it.

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:15.140
We will only grow over that.

00:11:15.140 --> 00:11:17.700
And of course, you can imagine
security in a hospital,

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.160
this is a prime thing.

00:11:19.160 --> 00:11:20.960
We have very complex
transactions.

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:24.170
And just to give you an
idea, into that system,

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:29.900
we have about 7,000 tables
with about 5,000 triggers

00:11:29.900 --> 00:11:33.590
that have 300,000
lines of SQL code,

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:37.340
and managing all that
complexity of transactions.

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:38.840
Another big thing
that we have is we

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:42.260
do weekly releases, consistently
over the last 25 years.

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:43.820
And we want to keep
that because it's

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:46.670
very important for all
things of new ideas

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:49.190
pop up in the health care.

00:11:49.190 --> 00:11:50.990
Also, in health care,
deletionless data

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:52.200
is very important.

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:55.370
Every data that goes in
never gets deleted, updated,

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:56.070
or anything.

00:11:56.070 --> 00:11:57.620
It just needs to remain there.

00:11:57.620 --> 00:12:00.470
We need to know at
every moment in time

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:02.780
what the status of
the patient was,

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:05.991
and we need to go back for that.

00:12:05.991 --> 00:12:07.490
And we have no
maintenance, we know.

00:12:07.490 --> 00:12:12.470
As I just said, you can be down
for one, two, to three minutes.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:14.190
But going down for
longer than that

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:16.310
is not an option in a hospital.

00:12:16.310 --> 00:12:18.980
Of course, this is a
big challenge for us.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:22.190
And so we want that burden
somebody else's problem,

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:23.550
and not our problem anymore.

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.909
So we looked around,
and what can we do?

00:12:25.909 --> 00:12:27.950
And just to give you an
idea of what we currently

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:31.305
do with our system-- so we
have that one big database,

00:12:31.305 --> 00:12:32.000
as I said.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.340
But of course, technically,
it's all a set up

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:38.780
of three logical servers
being logically replicated

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:41.780
to each other, just to make sure
that one is the primary server.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:43.880
But whenever
something goes wrong,

00:12:43.880 --> 00:12:46.970
you can quickly switch
to another server.

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.690
And then the user
will not see that.

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:54.140
But also, it's structurally
done for doing maintenance,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:57.050
because, as I say, we don't
have a maintenance window.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.990
But that says that
the user doesn't want

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:01.160
to see a maintenance window.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.110
But of course you,
as a DB admin,

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:04.340
you need to do maintenance.

00:13:04.340 --> 00:13:05.390
So how do you do that?

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:07.940
We keep those three
servers up and running.

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:10.820
So one is the primary, and the
two other are hot standbys.

00:13:10.820 --> 00:13:13.370
But not only that, we
also have warm standbys

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:16.460
because those hot standbys,
they are hot standbys.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:18.260
It means that whenever
you do something

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.360
logically wrong on
the primary server,

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:25.190
it automatically gets replicated
within seconds, or at most

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.840
a minute, to the other servers.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:30.500
And then the accident is gone,
and you do not have the data.

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:34.400
So therefore, we currently have
that setup of warm standbys

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:38.450
where you lag about
an hour, or a day,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:40.090
and to make sure
that you can just

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:44.090
retrieve in case of a
logical error the data there.

00:13:44.090 --> 00:13:46.070
And then apart from
that, of course,

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:49.010
you have all the other
copies for doing development,

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.740
for doing testing, for
doing training, et cetera,

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:56.160
read-only for clinical
trials, and so on and so on.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:14:00.730
So we now envision
a new application.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:02.870
And the application is not
just for the hospitals,

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:06.540
but we're immediately going to
attract also the first line.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:11.740
So we're also going to go to
the primary care, so the family

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:15.560
doctors, GPs, home nursing,
et cetera, et cetera,

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:18.740
into that system, so all
your data is automatically

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:19.680
connected.

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:21.110
We call it the MOAPR.

00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.690
It's a very, very
unsexy name, I know,

00:14:23.690 --> 00:14:26.180
but its an acronym for
something that stuck inside.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:29.430
And we say its the Mother
Of All Patient Records.

00:14:29.430 --> 00:14:30.980
So that's what we're
trying to build.

00:14:30.980 --> 00:14:35.240
And therefore, we needed a
big, stable, and good solution

00:14:35.240 --> 00:14:36.170
for the back end.

00:14:36.170 --> 00:14:38.060
So what is our
current architecture

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:39.120
that we're building?

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:42.680
In the front end, we have
a Angular environment.

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:46.340
Talks in the middle tier
to a GO middle tier.

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.460
It's all nicely
connected with the GRPC.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:52.340
And in the Google
Cloud Platform,

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:56.930
it's always very nice to have
that reiterations over and over

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:59.840
again from your APIs,
as we are currently

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.090
developing continuously.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:05.880
And in the back, it's driven
by Cloud Spanner, of course.

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:09.320
Apart from that, we
also have pops up

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.940
to go to the Google BigQuery.

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:13.610
And there, we do
currently our requirement

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:15.920
of the deletionless data
so that we can always

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:19.640
track all versions of the
data that were ever there.

00:15:19.640 --> 00:15:23.510
Of course, our DBAs, as typical
DBAs of an on-prem environment

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:28.070
are, they're very scary
about the cloud solution.

00:15:28.070 --> 00:15:30.350
They say, well, will
this really work?

00:15:30.350 --> 00:15:32.660
So they did a lot of
extensive performance testing,

00:15:32.660 --> 00:15:35.770
and so on, and so on, to make
sure that, yes, this will work.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:37.440
And so they just give
me some overviews

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:38.820
of testing that they did.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.500
And they basically
said, whatever

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:44.200
we tried with Cloud Spanner,
it just scales linearly.

00:15:44.200 --> 00:15:46.080
So no worries.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:47.310
This will work for us.

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:49.920
So we're very happy to have
been working with the team.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:54.252
And now, it's back
to you, Deepti.

00:15:54.252 --> 00:15:55.460
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Thank you.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:56.418
MARK VANAUTGAERDEN: OK.

