00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:17.840
But now, let's actually
move on to why you all

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:22.130
are here, which is to learn
more about what's new.

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:26.150
So again, going back to our
product philosophy of building

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:30.740
what you all and our customers
have been asking for,

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:34.160
the first question I get asked,
and the second, and the tenth

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:36.720
is, what about
disaster recovery?

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:41.750
So I'm very happy to talk
about our launch last week

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:44.510
of our import/export
functionality.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:49.790
And it allows you to do a bunch
of different use cases, not

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:51.440
just disaster recovery.

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:55.280
But the highlights here are that
it's a UI functionality that's

00:16:55.280 --> 00:16:55.910
push-button.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:17:00.950
So you can take a consistent
dump of your Spanner database

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:03.530
and put it, by default,
in a GCS bucket,

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:07.280
using our Apache Beam-based
Dataflow connectors.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:11.119
And it's stored, by
default, in our Apache Avro

00:17:11.119 --> 00:17:14.060
format, which is, again
another open source format.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:15.920
And again, with the
push of a button,

00:17:15.920 --> 00:17:19.430
you can import the data
back into Cloud Spanner.

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:23.180
So what this does is allows
you to take your own backups,

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:25.790
at your convenience, and store
them in a different medium

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:30.890
to get even better
separation of concerns here.

00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:33.260
It also enables a couple
of other interesting use

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.300
cases, which is you
can actually take

00:17:35.300 --> 00:17:38.030
this dump, this export of
your production Cloud Spanner

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:40.602
database, and use it for
dev test environments.

00:17:40.602 --> 00:17:42.560
So you can load it into
a different environment

00:17:42.560 --> 00:17:44.080
for dev and testing.

00:17:44.080 --> 00:17:46.640
You can do database
configuration moves.

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:50.120
What I mean by that is you can
export a Cloud Spanner data out

00:17:50.120 --> 00:17:52.010
and put it in a
different configuration.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:53.690
Because ultimately,
you can import it

00:17:53.690 --> 00:17:57.780
into any Cloud Spanner
configuration that you want.

00:17:57.780 --> 00:18:00.860
So it allows you to go from
one configuration to another.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:11.250
It also allows you to do
things, like export it out,

00:18:11.250 --> 00:18:16.710
and then allow BigQuery to
do analytics on your database

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:17.230
as well.

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:18.880
So it makes it a
little bit easier.

00:18:18.880 --> 00:18:20.380
So you don't have to
ETL it, necessarily.

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:21.810
You can just dump
it out, and then

00:18:21.810 --> 00:18:26.580
have BigQuery just point it to
a GCS bucket of your choice.

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:29.670
And it'll put the
data into BigQuery.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.250
And then-- my
