functionality,

00:23:18.130 --> 00:23:21.550
which we released last week.

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:26.420
The next question out, maybe the
11th, is more about DML, right?

00:23:26.420 --> 00:23:33.239
So this is the ability to
do SQL queries in Spanner.

00:23:33.239 --> 00:23:35.530
So Spanner allows you to do
INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE,

00:23:35.530 --> 00:23:38.740
obviously, but
through the RPC API.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:43.010
And so far, you
couldn't do it in SQL.

00:23:43.010 --> 00:23:45.760
But I am very happy
to say that we're

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:49.060
working on this functionality,
so that you could

00:23:49.060 --> 00:23:52.960
do SQL queries inside Scanner.

00:23:52.960 --> 00:23:55.720
So what this means
is that you can now

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:59.560
use your existing and
standard traditional tooling

00:23:59.560 --> 00:24:03.070
for databases, including JDBC
drivers and other drivers

00:24:03.070 --> 00:24:04.450
and ORMs.

00:24:04.450 --> 00:24:06.970
And Spanner's
looking and feeling

00:24:06.970 --> 00:24:10.210
and smelling a lot more like
your traditional databases.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:12.850
So with this functionality,
you can do INSERT, UPDATE,

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:13.810
and DELETE in SQL.

00:24:13.810 --> 00:24:16.150
You can do complex queries.

00:24:16.150 --> 00:24:18.490
We will have this functionality,
like with all Spanner

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:22.750
functionality almost, we'll
it in the UI, CLI, and API.

00:24:22.750 --> 00:24:26.770
And when we release
DML, we will also

00:24:26.770 --> 00:24:29.630
support it in the JDBC
driver that we already have.

00:24:29.630 --> 00:24:32.650
So again, this is not about any
timeline or anything like that,

00:24:32.650 --> 00:24:36.970
I just wanted to show you that
what we're working on and to--

00:24:36.970 --> 00:24:41.600
again, why talk about it when
you can see it in action?

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:46.372
So Steve is going to help
me again with this demo,

00:24:46.372 --> 00:24:47.580
if we can switch the screens.

