This time, again, we're
back to the query console.

00:24:54.100 --> 00:24:57.020
And we are looking at
a different database.

00:24:57.020 --> 00:24:58.960
So hang on.

00:24:58.960 --> 00:25:01.570
So we're looking at a different
database this time, which

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:03.020
is a ticket info database.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:05.478
So this is a little bit more
interesting than the YCSB one.

00:25:05.478 --> 00:25:07.120
As you can see on
the side, the schema

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:11.290
shows that we have Event table,
Account table, Ticket table,

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:12.250
et cetera.

00:25:12.250 --> 00:25:14.740
So it's a fairly
standard database.

00:25:14.740 --> 00:25:19.100
And Event table here has
two indices, as well.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:23.800
So let's run a query to
see what events we have.

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:28.700
So in the query console,
you can run SQL queries.

00:25:28.700 --> 00:25:31.384
And you can see the
results right here.

00:25:31.384 --> 00:25:32.800
And as you can
see, the main thing

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:36.620
here is that there isn't
any multi-event IDs set.

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:39.520
So if we go to the
explanation, actually, you

00:25:39.520 --> 00:25:42.860
can see that it's using
an event by date index.

00:25:42.860 --> 00:25:45.100
So it's doing the
right thing to be

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:47.240
running the query efficiently.

00:25:47.240 --> 00:25:50.560
And if we go back,
now, let's do something

00:25:50.560 --> 00:25:53.020
interesting with
this table, which

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:57.360
is to change the multi-event
ID for all events that started

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:01.400
yesterday to Cloud Next, because
Cloud Next started yesterday.

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:06.560
So-- ooh, DML query completed.

00:26:06.560 --> 00:26:10.180
And again, if you look
at the query plan,

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:14.300
now we support DML
operations in the query

00:26:14.300 --> 00:26:16.120
we plan to apply mutation.

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:18.280
Sorry, I hope you can see this.

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:22.630
And just to make sure that
we're not fooling you,

00:26:22.630 --> 00:26:25.030
let's actually see
the Event table again.

00:26:25.030 --> 00:26:28.540
And hopefully, we'll
see a multi-event ID

00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:30.010
being updated for
all events that

00:26:30.010 --> 00:26:34.530
are starting as of yesterday.

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:36.520
Look.

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:37.600
It got updated.

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:38.890
Woo-hoo!

00:26:38.890 --> 00:26:41.230
So DML actually works.

00:26:41.230 --> 00:26:43.840
And now, let's do something a
little bit more interesting,

00:26:43.840 --> 00:26:46.620
because I have a bunch of
customers who say, well,

00:26:46.620 --> 00:26:48.340
but will you be able
to do conditionals,

00:26:48.340 --> 00:26:49.930
and WHERE clauses,
and IF clauses,

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.310
and all that sort of stuff?

00:26:51.310 --> 00:26:53.650
In your distributed database
that can, by the way,

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:57.250
scale to petabyte-scale
databases.

00:26:57.250 --> 00:27:01.902
So here, we have
the Attendees table.

00:27:01.902 --> 00:27:03.860
So basically, we have a
list of attendees here.

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:06.250
And what we want to do
is mark all the attendees

00:27:06.250 --> 00:27:08.770
that are coming to my
session today VIPs,

00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:10.120
because we like you all.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:14.222
So here, we can see that
Alice and Carol are attending

00:27:14.222 --> 00:27:15.680
the Spanish session,
so we're going

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:18.210
to mark them as VIPs for
the rest of the event.

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:18.940
Now, disclaimer.

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:23.030
This doesn't actually hook into
the actual events for today.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.330
So if you are Alice
and Carol, you

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:28.040
won't be a VIP, unless
you already are.

00:27:28.040 --> 00:27:30.290
But the idea here
is that we should

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:32.800
be able to update this
with the conditional

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:34.270
and add the VIP tag.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:40.380
So let's actually look at the
query again and see if we can--

00:27:43.796 --> 00:27:47.360
[LAUGHS] It doesn't matter.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:49.650
Live demo, people.

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:58.272
So basically, we should update
this Attendees table with--

00:27:58.272 --> 00:28:03.050
[LAUGHS] with VIP status
for Alice and Carol.

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:03.620
Perfect.

00:28:03.620 --> 00:28:04.550
It works.

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:08.840
And we demonstrated that
we are canned, right?

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:12.360
So that gives you a whirlwind
tour of our DML functionality

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:13.460
that we're working on.

00:28:13.460 --> 00:28:16.770
It should, as I said,
give you a look and feel

00:28:16.770 --> 00:28:18.830
of Spanner, similar
to other databases

00:28:18.830 --> 00:28:21.800
and should make it easier
for you to both migrate

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:25.370
and then use it with
your existing tooling.

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:33.890
So moving on, another thing
that is frequently asked

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:35.620
is performance tuning.

00:28:35.620 --> 00:28:37.620
So this is common
for all databases.

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:39.520
Everybody wants more--

00:28:39.520 --> 00:28:43.730
[LAUGHS] I can
high-five you as well.

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:46.910
Everybody wants more
insight into their database,

00:28:46.910 --> 00:28:48.410
because they know
they can do better

00:28:48.410 --> 00:28:50.326
than what the database
is doing automatically,

00:28:50.326 --> 00:28:53.260
no matter what we think about
our heuristic algorithms.

00:28:53.260 --> 00:28:57.590
So we're working on another
exciting new feature

00:28:57.590 --> 00:29:02.250
that I'm going to have Anna
step up here and help me demo.

00:29:02.250 --> 00:29:04.400
Anna is one of the lead
engineers for this feature.

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:06.290
It's called Query Statistics.

00:29:06.290 --> 00:29:08.730
Again, this is something
that's coming soon.

00:29:08.730 --> 00:29:11.690
We're planning to release
it later this year.

00:29:11.690 --> 00:29:15.590
But I'm very excited about
it because it gives you

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:18.380
introspection into your
worst performing queries.

00:29:18.380 --> 00:29:21.200
And there are multiple
additional dimensions

00:29:21.200 --> 00:29:23.180
along which we report stats.

00:29:23.180 --> 00:29:25.700
So you can see what's
going on with your queries.

00:29:25.700 --> 00:29:29.810
And of course, we'll have this
available in our API and our UI

00:29:29.810 --> 00:29:31.560
in multiple granularity buckets.

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:35.900
So let's see it in action
if you could switch, please.

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.680
So we're back to our UI console.

00:29:39.680 --> 00:29:42.000
Now, we're just doing
it in the UI console

00:29:42.000 --> 00:29:44.390
because it's easier
to see and understand.

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:47.310
But of course, we'll have it
programmatically, as well.

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:50.900
So this is the same
ticket info schema

00:29:50.900 --> 00:29:55.070
that we saw earlier with the
account, and event, and venue,

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:56.060
and whatnot.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:59.210
But this time, we have an
exciting new tab called,

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:00.830
Query Statistics.

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:03.830
So what you can see when you
get into the Query Statistic tab

00:30:03.830 --> 00:30:07.070
is all of the queries that
ran in the last minute,

00:30:07.070 --> 00:30:09.405
in this case, that are
hogging the most CPU.

00:30:09.405 --> 00:30:11.030
So they're the most
resource-intensive,

00:30:11.030 --> 00:30:13.830
and we're looking at
sorting them per CPU.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:16.520
And where Anna's cursor
is is basically we

00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:18.555
have multiple buckets in
which you can see that.

00:30:18.555 --> 00:30:20.930
So you can not only see what
happened in the last minute,

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:23.540
but the last hour, the
last 24 hours, et cetera.

00:30:23.540 --> 00:30:26.540
But we're going to
stick to the last minute

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:29.330
and see our top query.

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.310
So if you click
on it, we actually

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:35.150
give you a lot more information
on the side in our Info panel.

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:39.357
And as you can see, the
query took 261 CPU seconds.

00:30:39.357 --> 00:30:40.940
It'll give you a lot
more information,

00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:45.410
like latency, rows
scanned, and rows sent.

00:30:45.410 --> 00:30:47.395
Now, in this case,
they're both the same,

00:30:47.395 --> 00:30:49.770
which means that the query
was not doing any aggregations

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:50.600
or filtering.

00:30:50.600 --> 00:30:53.060
And if you look at
the handy-dandy query

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:55.250
text on the top, you
can see that, indeed, it

00:30:55.250 --> 00:30:57.890
was not doing any
aggregations and filtering.

00:30:57.890 --> 00:31:01.089
But it is a parameterized query.

00:31:01.089 --> 00:31:02.880
And this is a good time
to talk about using

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:05.480
parameterized queries,
because they actually

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:07.430
make your queries
perform more efficiently,

00:31:07.430 --> 00:31:11.930
because the query engine
can store the query plan

00:31:11.930 --> 00:31:14.990
and not have to rebuild it
every time you run the query.

00:31:14.990 --> 00:31:16.670
It also gives us
better statistics

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:18.560
for things like query stats.

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:23.480
And even if you invoke
the query multiple times,

00:31:23.480 --> 00:31:26.630
it will store the
information for you

00:31:26.630 --> 00:31:29.480
because it's using
the same plan.

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:32.420
So in this case, the query was
run in the last minute 150,000

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:34.160
times, almost.

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:37.700
And all of that is
aggregated and put together

00:31:37.700 --> 00:31:40.080
in this Query Statistics.

00:31:40.080 --> 00:31:42.590
So let's look at a
more interesting query.

00:31:42.590 --> 00:31:47.450
So let's look at all the
venues by seating capacity.

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:51.140
And in this case, you can see
that the total CPU here taken

00:31:51.140 --> 00:31:54.410
was 24 seconds,
but the average CPU

00:31:54.410 --> 00:31:56.750
is much, much less than that.

00:31:56.750 --> 00:31:59.480
And what that means
is the Spanner, which

00:31:59.480 --> 00:32:03.830
is meant to distribute, is meant
to be parallelizing queries,

00:32:03.830 --> 00:32:06.620
is actually doing that, and
so giving you the response

00:32:06.620 --> 00:32:09.260
in a much shorter time.

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:11.370
Even though the execution
count is much smaller,

00:32:11.370 --> 00:32:15.570
so it's just another query
in your top 10 queries.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:17.750
So what if you wanted
to debug this query

00:32:17.750 --> 00:32:20.070
and see what it
did in real time?

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:25.950
So you can click on this button
to open into our Query Console.

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:27.770
Very cool, I think.

00:32:27.770 --> 00:32:31.310
We are database geeks,
so what we think is cool

00:32:31.310 --> 00:32:33.030
is not necessarily
cool for everybody.

00:32:33.030 --> 00:32:36.770
But as you can see here,
the query ran, in this case.

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:42.270
And it gave us a list of all the
venues with seating capacity.

00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:43.874
And if you wanted
to debug it, you

00:32:43.874 --> 00:32:46.040
would want to look at the
query explanation as well,

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:47.780
to see what it's doing, right?

00:32:47.780 --> 00:32:51.320
And this is the explain
plan for this particular run

00:32:51.320 --> 00:32:52.462
of the query, of course.

00:32:52.462 --> 00:32:54.920
And you can see it's doing all
the things you expect it to.

00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.260
It's doing a hash and
then an aggregation

00:32:57.260 --> 00:33:00.760
to return the results to
you in descending order.

00:33:00.760 --> 00:33:01.570
Cool.

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:08.482
So hopefully, that was a
fun demo for you all of what

00:33:08.482 --> 00:33:09.190
we're working on.

00:33:09.190 --> 00:33:11.606
Thank you, Anna and Steve, for
running those demos for us.

00:33:11.606 --> 00:33:14.430
And if we can
switch back, great.

00:33:14.430 --> 00:33:18.700
So those are some very
exciting features-- one

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:21.400
of the two exciting features
that we're working on

00:33:21.400 --> 00:33:24.190
that I wanted to show to you
all to make sure you understand

00:33:24.190 --> 00:33:27.140
that we are listening to you
and we are working on things.

00:33:27.140 --> 00:33:29.020
And we plan to
release both of these

00:33:29.020 --> 00:33:34.120
in the next couple of months.

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:39.250
So before we end, I wanted to
also address a couple of points

00:33:39.250 --> 00:33:43.540
that I've heard over the past
year that we've been around

00:33:43.540 --> 00:33:47.612
and talk about some of the
myths that I see happening.

00:33:47.612 --> 00:33:49.570
So I wanted to do a little
bit of myth busting.

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:57.460
So is Cloud Spanner really the
right database for your app?

00:33:57.460 --> 00:33:58.300
I hear this a lot.

00:33:58.300 --> 00:34:01.810
You know, Cloud Spanner is meant
only for very large workloads.

00:34:01.810 --> 00:34:03.730
And the fact is that
Spanner is actually

00:34:03.730 --> 00:34:06.370
built so that you
can start with it

00:34:06.370 --> 00:34:08.770
and never have to re-platform.

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:10.540
The idea is fire and forget.

00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:12.460
You start with it,
and then you never

00:34:12.460 --> 00:34:16.130
have to think about
sharding or which

00:34:16.130 --> 00:34:19.690
scale, versus performance,
versus name your thing

00:34:19.690 --> 00:34:23.980
that you care about,
because it can just

00:34:23.980 --> 00:34:25.670
grow with your application.

00:34:25.670 --> 00:34:28.429
So you want to focus on
getting the next billion users.

00:34:28.429 --> 00:34:29.860
We're right there with you.

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:32.980
We can help you grow
as you grow, right?

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:35.090
In fact, what we've
seen is that--

00:34:35.090 --> 00:34:37.780
and this is a known statistic--
like most average enterprise

00:34:37.780 --> 00:34:41.020
database sizes are
hundreds of gigabytes

00:34:41.020 --> 00:34:43.280
and up to a terabyte or two.

00:34:43.280 --> 00:34:45.120
And average Cloud
Spanner database sizes

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:47.110
are right smack in the
middle of that, right?

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:50.800
We have one database that is
35 terabytes, and it's growing.

00:34:50.800 --> 00:34:51.770
Great.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:55.810
But the idea is that you can use
Spanner for the entire spectrum

00:34:55.810 --> 00:34:58.690
because you're getting
the benefit of a managed

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:01.690
database that is scalable,
that is parallelizable,

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:06.460
that is secure,
all in one package.

00:35:06.460 --> 00:35:08.320
We have regional
configurations and

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:10.720
multi-region configurations.

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:13.514
Whichever one you use is great.

00:35:13.514 --> 00:35:15.680
Multi-region configurations
offer high availability,

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:17.396
if that's what you want.

00:35:17.396 --> 00:35:19.270
And they can lower
latency, so you can use it

00:35:19.270 --> 00:35:24.070
as a content distribution system
for your transactional data,

00:35:24.070 --> 00:35:26.680
if that's what you want.

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:28.420
But that has nothing
to do with size.

00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:30.100
It's to do with
your requirements

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:32.650
and making the right choice
for your requirements.

00:35:32.650 --> 00:35:35.590
And also, as I said, with the
Import/Export functionality,

00:35:35.590 --> 00:35:37.965
for example, if you started
out with a regional instance,

00:35:37.965 --> 00:35:40.256
and you thought, oh, I actually
need to be multi-region

00:35:40.256 --> 00:35:41.800
because, you know,
I'm now popular

00:35:41.800 --> 00:35:42.799
everywhere in the world.

00:35:42.799 --> 00:35:45.610
And I really want to
grow my health care

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:47.074
business all over the world.

00:35:47.074 --> 00:35:48.490
Then you can just
take a snapshot,

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:51.280
move into a multi-region
configuration.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:56.170
So Cloud Spanner is being
used for typical enterprise

00:35:56.170 --> 00:35:59.140
and other database
workloads, and we can expect

00:35:59.140 --> 00:36:02.830
for it to continue to do that.

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:05.320
The other thing I
hear quite often

00:36:05.320 --> 00:36:07.000
is Cloud Spanner is expensive.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:13.030
And when you look at
total cost of ownership,

00:36:13.030 --> 00:36:16.540
actually, in most
cases, Cloud Scanner

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:20.560
actually gives you great
value for your money

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:23.260
because it's fully managed.

00:36:23.260 --> 00:36:26.492
You don't have to have teams
of people carrying pagers--

00:36:26.492 --> 00:36:28.450
or if you're a startup
and you have one person,

00:36:28.450 --> 00:36:30.970
that person carrying
pagers all the time.

00:36:30.970 --> 00:36:32.480
There's no planned downtime.

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:36.580
There's no patches or upgrades
to be added, or whatever.

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:39.310
It's high availability.

00:36:39.310 --> 00:36:40.930
It's replicated.

00:36:40.930 --> 00:36:44.020
And it has security and
compliance built-in.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:47.040
We don't nickel and dime
you and say, you get this.

00:36:47.040 --> 00:36:49.180
But if you want this,
you have to pay me

00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:52.570
so much more, and then so much
more, and so much more, right?

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:54.310
We want to be open
and transparent

00:36:54.310 --> 00:36:57.400
and give you the best
platform for your applications

00:36:57.400 --> 00:36:59.590
and not have to have
you worry about what

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:02.050
is my monthly bill
going to look like.

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:06.370
And of course, storage
auto-scales, so you never

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:07.690
have to provision storage.

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:08.770
And more importantly,
you don't have

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:11.269
to bring your entire database
down to provision the storage,

00:37:11.269 --> 00:37:12.220
right?

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:15.610
And you can scale up and
down the compute as you need,

00:37:15.610 --> 00:37:17.440
as you want to go.

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:19.670
So you can actually
increase compute on-demand,

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:22.390
if you have expected
high load because it's

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:24.910
Black Friday, or whichever,
Mother's Day, or whatever.

00:37:24.910 --> 00:37:28.600
And you can tune
it down when you

00:37:28.600 --> 00:37:33.445
need for a lower number
of nodes if you need that.

00:37:33.445 --> 00:37:38.290
So you have elastic
compute, and you

00:37:38.290 --> 00:37:41.560
have auto-scaling storage
in a fully managed,

00:37:41.560 --> 00:37:46.510
secure, compliant database.

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:49.330
And of course, Brad mentioned
this earlier in the keynote

